 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gce-as-and-a-level-mathematics",
 "standard_text": "Differentiate using the product rule, the quotient rule and the chain rule; use differentiation to find gradients, tangents and stationary points.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 13 A level maths worksheets on the product rule and the quotient rule: differentiating products and quotients of polynomials, eˣ, ln x, sin x and cos x, gradients, stationary points and tangents. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Product: (uv)′ = u′v + uv′.",
 "key_sub": "Quotient: (u/v)′ = (vu′ − uv′)/v².",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>For <i>y</i> = <i>uv</i>: d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = <i>u</i>′<i>v</i> + <i>uv</i>′. For <i>y</i> = <i>u</i>/<i>v</i>: d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = (<i>vu</i>′ − <i>uv</i>′)/<i>v</i>². Write down <i>u</i>, <i>v</i>, <i>u</i>′ and <i>v</i>′ first.</p><p>Factorise the answer (for example take out e<sup><i>x</i></sup>) to find stationary points: e<sup><i>x</i></sup> is never 0, so set the other factor to 0.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Differentiate <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>²e<sup><i>x</i></sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>u</i>′<i>v</i> = 2<i>x</i>e<sup><i>x</i></sup>, <i>uv</i>′ = <i>x</i>²e<sup><i>x</i></sup></p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i>e<sup><i>x</i></sup>(<i>x</i> + 2)</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Differentiate <i>y</i> = (2<i>x</i> + 1)/(<i>x</i> + 3).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>[2(<i>x</i> + 3) − 1(2<i>x</i> + 1)]/(<i>x</i> + 3)²</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5/(<i>x</i> + 3)²</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying the two derivatives: (uv)′ is not u′v′.</li><li>Getting the order wrong in the quotient rule (uv′ − vu′).</li><li>Forgetting to square the denominator.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the product rule?",
   "a": "If y = uv, then dy/dx = u′v + uv′: differentiate one factor at a time and add."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the quotient rule?",
   "a": "If y = u/v, then dy/dx = (vu′ − uv′)/v². The minus sign means the order matters."
  }
 ],
